From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Platon <nxt8787@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 11 Jun 2026 16:51:03 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] 9269: Fix StackOverflowError in BatchInfo when filesystem
 contains cycles

The batch importer descends into filesystems via two recursive paths in
BatchInfo.doAddFile():

  1. file.isDirectory() -> processFS(file.getFilesystem(), file, ...)
  2. processAsFS(fsrl) -> shouldTerminateRecurse() -> processFS(fs, ...)

Path 2 is guarded by the maxDepth check in shouldTerminateRecurse(), but
path 1 (the directory case) has no depth guard at all. When a filesystem
contains a cycle - an NTFS junction pointing at an ancestor, a symlink
loop, a self-referencing archive container, or a directory tree with
back-references - the doAddFile -> processFS -> doAddFile chain recurses
without bound and the JVM eventually crashes:

  java.lang.StackOverflowError
      at ghidra.formats.gfilesystem.FileSystemRef.<init>(...)
      at ghidra.formats.gfilesystem.FileSystemRefManager.create(...)
      at ghidra.plugins.importer.batch.BatchInfo.doAddFile(BatchInfo.java:226)
      at ghidra.plugins.importer.batch.BatchInfo.processFS(BatchInfo.java:352)
      [repeating thousands of times]

This change adds two complementary defences inside doAddFile():

  - A Set<FSRL> visitedFSRLs tracks every FSRL the batch session has
    already processed. If doAddFile() is called with an FSRL it has
    already seen, it logs a warning and returns immediately, breaking
    the cycle. This is the precise fix: it identifies the actual cycle
    by FSRL equality, regardless of how many levels deep the recursion
    has gone.

  - A static HARD_MAX_NEST_DEPTH (32) backstops the FSRL set in the
    unlikely case that an adversarial filesystem produces an unbounded
    stream of distinct FSRLs that never repeat. Path 2's maxDepth check
    is bypassed when the user constructs BatchInfo with MAXDEPTH_UNLIMITED
    (-1) or 0, so the static cap is the only guarantee against stack
    exhaustion in that mode.

Both guards write a single Msg.warn() so the user sees why a subtree
was skipped, rather than silently dropping files or crashing.

Backwards compatible: no public API changes, no behaviour change for
inputs that do not cycle. The Set is allocated once per BatchInfo
instance and only ever grows for the lifetime of that session.

Reproducer: Batch Import on a directory containing an NTFS junction
that points back at the directory itself, or any container whose
internal filesystem references an ancestor.
---
 .../plugins/importer/batch/BatchInfo.java     | 37 +++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 37 insertions(+)

diff --git a/Ghidra/Features/Base/src/main/java/ghidra/plugins/importer/batch/BatchInfo.java b/Ghidra/Features/Base/src/main/java/ghidra/plugins/importer/batch/BatchInfo.java
index 7850788e53..41be66cf3f 100644
--- a/Ghidra/Features/Base/src/main/java/ghidra/plugins/importer/batch/BatchInfo.java
+++ b/Ghidra/Features/Base/src/main/java/ghidra/plugins/importer/batch/BatchInfo.java
@@ -60,6 +60,23 @@ public class BatchInfo {
 	private List<UserAddedSourceInfo> userAddedSources =
 		Collections.synchronizedList(new ArrayList<>());
 
+	/**
+	 * Tracks FSRLs already visited during the current batch session. Used to
+	 * detect filesystem cycles (symlink loops, self-referencing archives,
+	 * directory junctions, etc.) and prevent infinite recursion that would
+	 * otherwise cause {@link StackOverflowError} in the {@code doAddFile} /
+	 * {@code processFS} chain.
+	 */
+	private Set<FSRL> visitedFSRLs = Collections.synchronizedSet(new HashSet<>());
+
+	/**
+	 * Hard upper bound on filesystem nesting depth, enforced regardless of
+	 * {@link #maxDepth}. Belt-and-suspenders for cases where FSRL identity
+	 * comparison fails to detect a cycle (e.g., FSRLs that grow without ever
+	 * repeating).
+	 */
+	private static final int HARD_MAX_NEST_DEPTH = 32;
+
 	/**
 	 * Maximum depth of containers (ie. filesystems) to recurse into when processing
 	 * a file added by the user.
@@ -220,6 +237,26 @@ public class BatchInfo {
 
 		// use the fsrl param instead of file.getFSRL() as param may have more info (ie. md5)
 
+		// Cycle detection: if this FSRL was already visited in the current batch
+		// session, skip it to break filesystem cycles (symlink loops, self-
+		// referencing archives, NTFS junctions, deep directory trees with
+		// back-references, etc.). Without this, doAddFile -> processFS ->
+		// doAddFile can recurse without bound and crash with StackOverflowError.
+		if (!visitedFSRLs.add(fsrl)) {
+			Msg.warn(this,
+				"BatchInfo: cycle detected, skipping already-visited FSRL: " + fsrl);
+			return false;
+		}
+
+		// Hard depth fallback: even if FSRL identity check misses a cycle (e.g.,
+		// FSRLs that grow without ever repeating), enforce an absolute cap so a
+		// pathological filesystem cannot consume the entire JVM stack.
+		if (fsrl.getNestingDepth() > HARD_MAX_NEST_DEPTH) {
+			Msg.warn(this, "BatchInfo: hard nesting depth limit (" +
+				HARD_MAX_NEST_DEPTH + ") reached, skipping: " + fsrl);
+			return false;
+		}
+
 		try (RefdFile refdFile = fsService.getRefdFile(fsrl, taskMonitor)) {
 			GFile file = refdFile.file;
 			if (file.isDirectory()) {
